a1b64b-fe4d-73dd-9fb2-26f680d0b3a8,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b64c02387d24acacbd4a13028c46"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T13:57:12.720Z"}
{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b64c-8da0-7774-b507-6ebd5d4f675b","session_id":"01a1b64b-fe4d-73dd-9fb2-26f680d0b3a8","message":{"id":"01a1b64c8da07774b5076ebbaace9143","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Design's mapping matches the task-graph (T-016 accepts 036,037,039,040; design table says AC-036..040 T-016 ✓; T-022 accepts 051-056 ✓; T-024 059,060,061 ✓; T-025 062,063 ✓; T-029 038,069 ✓; T-030 070,071 ✓; T-003 006,007,008,072 ✓; T-004 009,010,011,073 ✓).\n\nNow let me check the acceptance-matrix.md levels to verify the table (design says levels come from acceptance-matrix). Let me spot-check AC-072 and AC-073 levels from task-graph above: AC-072 unit, AC-073 contract,unit ✓. AC-008 contract,integration ✓. AC-029 integration,unit ✓. AC-031 integration,unit ✓. AC-053 contract,unit ✓. AC-066 e2e,integration ✓.\n\nLet me verify a few ACs in the acceptance matrix for levels differences. Actually design says \"owner 与 levels：逐行取自蓝图 acceptance-matrix.md（levels 列），与 task-graph.json 一致\". task-graph AC list has levels. Let me use task-graph levels, which I can read fully from lines 25-553. I saw most. Let me check AC-059 (integration), AC-060 (integration), AC-061 (integration,unit) ✓ matches design. AC-067 integration ✓, AC-068 unit ✓, AC-070 unit ✓, AC-071 integration ✓, AC-069 smoke ✓, AC-038 smoke ✓, AC-064 e2e ✓, AC-065 e2e ✓, AC-066 e2e,integration ✓.\n\nDesign data table lists:\n- AC-012 T-005 integration,unit ✓ (task-graph: integration,unit)\n- AC-011 T-004 contract,unit ✓\n- AC-013 T-005 unit ✓\n- AC-030 T-011 unit ✓ (task-graph AC-030 unit)\n- AC-031 T-012 integration,unit ✓ — wait design table says AC-031 T-012 integration,unit. Task-graph T-012 acceptance AC-031 ✓, levels integration,unit ✓.\n- AC-032 T-013 integration ✓\n- AC-033 T-013 unit ✓\n- AC-053 T-022 contract,unit ✓\n- AC-056 T-022 contract ✓\n- AC-061 T-024 integration,unit ✓\n- AC-063 T-025 integration ✓\n- AC-062 T-025 unit ✓\n- AC-073 T-004 contract,unit ✓\n\nGood, design table is consistent.\n\nBaseline defaults: [\"darwin\",\"linux\",\"win32\"], exceptions AC-027 win32, AC-033 linux, AC-038 real-smoke, AC-069 linux.\n\nBaseline compatibility: levels containing unit/contract/integration/e2e → baseline must not include real-smoke. levels containing smoke → baseline must not include win32/darwin. AC-038: levels [smoke], baseline [real-smoke] ✓. AC-069: [smoke], [linux] ✓. AC-027: [integration], [win32] ✓. AC-033: [unit], [linux] ✓.\n\nNow I need to write a lot of code. Let me plan carefully. Key risks: no execution. I must be extremely careful with correctness.\n\nLet me now think about the acceptance checks in detail, especially tricky ones.\n\n### V-01 write scope\n`git status --porcelain --untracked-files=all` — must only show allowed paths, excluding `.collab/tasks/001/`. Note impl.md is in `.collab/tasks/001/`? The instruction says produce impl.md. Where? Task dir presumably `.collab/tasks/001/impl.md`. That's under `.collab/tasks/001/`, excluded. Good.\n\nAlso \"跑过 npm test 之后仓库里也不应多出 test-results/ 之类的文件\" — my run-tests default out-dir is under os.tmpdir, good.\n\n### A1-01/A1-02\n`$o = NewTmp` then `npm test` (which runs with default out dir). Then `run-tests.mjs --out-dir $o`. Then check `$o/files-executed.json`.\n\nNote: `$o` is created by NewTmp and empty. run-tests creates dir, deletes old manifest, truncates skips.jsonl, then runs. Expect mode full, filter empty string (A1-02 expects `full||true|...` — so `filter` must be `''` in full mode, not null!). Wait: design §3.5 says `filter` 为过滤值字符串或 `null`. But A1-02 expects `m.filter` to render as empty in join → `''` or `null` would render `null`. `[m.mode, m.filter, ...].join('|')` with filter null → \"full|null|...\". Expected \"full||true|...\". So filter must be `''` (empty string) in full mode. Design also says env `CLIWORKER_RUN_FILTER`（过滤值，完整运行为空串）. So reporter should use `process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_FILTER ?? null`? If env is set to '', then filter = '' (since '' ?? null = ''). Actually `process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_FILTER` when set to empty string is ''. `'' ?? null` → ''. Good. And when env missing → undefined → null. That matches both: \"环境变量缺失时：mode 取 filtered、filter 取 null\". And full run sets env to '' giving filter ''. \n\nCareful: on Windows, setting env var to '' via spawn may... Actually Node's spawn with env: {CLIWORKER_RUN_FILTER: ''} on Windows passes empty string fine (child sees ''). For posix, empty string is passed as ''. Fine.\n\nHmm, but in Node, `process.env.X = ''` in Windows child... Windows API allows empty env values. Node filters out undefined but keeps ''. OK.\n\nBut safer: reporter reads `process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_FILTER`; if it's undefined → null. If defined (even '') → value. Use:\n```js\nconst rawFilter = process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_FILTER\nconst filter = rawFilter === undefined ? null : rawFilter\n```\n\nmode: `process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_MODE === 'full' ? 'full' : 'filtered'` (default filtered).\n\nObject key order in manifest: A1-02 checks `Object.keys(m).sort().join(',')` = `files,filter,mode,node,platform`. So exactly these 5 keys. Order in JSON doesn't matter due to sort, but the JSON.stringify must include only these. I'll write in order mode, filter, files, platform, node.\n\n### reporter implementation\nnode:test custom reporter: module default export must be an async generator function that takes a source of events and yields strings. Signature: `async function* (source)`. Events are objects with `type` and `data`. In Node 20/22, events: 'test:start', 'test:pass', 'test:fail', 'test:diagnostic', 'test:coverage', 'test:stdout', 'test:plan', ... For file-level grouping, `data.file` exists on test:pass/fail/start events? Actually in Node 20+, each test event has `file` property when tests come from files. Also 'test:summary' maybe. Design says collect all events where `event.data.file` is string.\n\nNode's reporter API: the generator gets an async iterable of events. We can do:\n```js\nexport default async function* filesReporter(source) {\n  const files = new Set()\n  for await (const event of source) {\n    const f = event?.data?.file\n    if (typeof f === 'string') files.add(f)\n  }\n  ...yield JSON\n}\n```\nThat works and doesn't need to handle yields during stream.\n\nPath relativization: paths may be absolute or file URLs? In Node, `data.file` is typically an absolute path (on Windows like `C:\\...`). Some versions give `file:` URL? In Node 20, test reporter events include `file` as absolute path string. Design says \"收集所有 event.data.file 为字符串的事件的文件路径... 路径转成相对 CLIWORKER_ROOT\". If it's a file URL we'd need fileURLToPath. I'll handle both: if it starts with 'file:' convert via fileURLToPath.\n\nAlso normalize: use path.relative(root, p) and convert '\\\\' to '/'.\n\nDedupe+sorted.\n\nA1-02 expects `m.files.includes('test/unit/select.test.mjs')`.\n\n### The `--test-reporter` with custom path\n`process.execPath --test --test-reporter=spec --test-reporter-destination=stdout --test-reporter=<url> --test-reporter-destination=<file> <files...>`\n\nOn Windows, args passed via spawn array are fine.\n\nNote: when using `--test` with explicit file list, node runs each file. Note Node 20's `--test-reporter` accepts module specifier; file URL should work.\n\nImportant: does Node's test runner pick up test files' `test()` from `node:test` and emit events with `file`? Yes.\n\nPotential issue: Node 22 with multiple reporters requires destinations count match. We give spec→stdout and custom→file. Good.\n\nAnother: Node's test runner by default with explicit paths might still use \"test runner\" glob? No, explicit paths given.\n\n### verifyFullRun\n```js\nexport function verifyFullRun({ enumerated, manifest }) {\n  const errors = []\n  if (!manifest || manifest.mode !== 'full') errors.push({ id: 'discovery.mode_not_full', path: '' })\n  ...\n}\n```\nDesign: errors entries; output `FAIL <标识> <路径>`. I'll use objects {id, path}. check-test-discovery uses the same.\n\nRules:\n- mode !== 'full' → discovery.mode_not_full\n- enumerated not in manifest → discovery.missing\n- manifest files not enumerated → discovery.unexpected\n- enumerated empty → discovery.no_files\n\nA1-09 expects `manifest-missing.json` filled with enumerated = [a,b], manifest files=[a] → missing b → discovery.missing. manifest-extra → unexpected. manifest-filtered with same set → mode_not_full (and no diff errors).\n\nOrder of errors: when filtered with equal set, only mode_not_full. Good.\n\n### run-tests CLI\nArgs: --root, --out-dir, --ac (repeat), --perf, --update-snapshots.\n\nUnknown arg → exit 2 with usage on stderr.\n\n`--ac` value must match ^AC-\\d{3}$ else exit 2.\n\nNested guard: if `process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE` set AND resolved outDir === env CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR → exit 2. A1-11 sets both to $o and passes --out-dir $o → exit 2. Compare resolved absolute paths (case-insensitive on win32). I'll normalize via path.resolve and lowercase on win32.\n\nImportant: A1-11 sets CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR=$o and runs `--out-dir $o` → equal → exit 2. ✓\n\nBut careful: this guard must come before creating dir etc. Fine either way.\n\nNote nesting in milestone-gate: gate runs run-tests with --out-dir mkdtemp. If the outer process is itself a run-tests child (CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE=1, CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR=outer dir), the gate's out-dir differs → allowed. But wait: when npm test runs, the child test processes have CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE=1 and CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR=<default tmp dir>. Inside milestone-gate.test.mjs, we spawn milestone-gate, which spawns run-tests with a mkdtemp out-dir. If mkdtemp happens to be ... no, different. But the guard checks equality only. However there's another subtlety: the nested run-tests will inherit CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE=1; that's fine.\n\nHmm but wait — potential recursion problem: milestone-gate runs run-tests on the fixture root, and the fixture's tests are simple. OK.\n\nBut there IS a serious recursion issue: `npm test` runs all tests including milestone-gate.test.mjs, which spawns milestone-gate on fixtures; the gate then runs run-tests on the fixture root only. Fine, no recursion into repo tests.\n\nBut check-fast runs check-* scripts then npm test. check-fast is invoked in runner-selftest tests with injection variables so it doesn't recurse. ✓\n\n### per-file enumeration\nenumerateTestFiles(root):\n- dirs = unit, integration, contract, e2e, smoke, characterization, flows\n- recursive walk, skip fixtures/helpers/perf dirs (only relevant if present under those top dirs? Design says \"test/fixtures、test/helpers、test/perf 整个排除\" — these are excluded whole; since we only walk the 7 top dirs, they're not reachable anyway. But be safe: skip any directory named fixtures/helpers/perf? No — a `test/unit/fixtures` dir might legitimately hold fixtures. Design says whole exclude at test root level. Since we only descend from the 7 named top-level dirs, test/fixtures etc. never get visited. Fine.)\n\nfindOrphans(root): walk `<root>/test` recursively, skipping top-level fixtures/helpers/perf (and their subtrees); return *.test.mjs files that are NOT under the 7 dirs. So any test file directly in test/ or in other dirs (e.g. test/lib-check/x.test.mjs). Return relative paths, sorted.\n\nImplementation: \n```js\nfunction walk(dir, out) { ... }\nconst testDir = path.join(root, 'test')\nfor entries of testDir: if dir and name in ['fixtures','helpers','perf'] skip; if dir recurse collecting all *.test.mjs; if file and endsWith('.test.mjs') → orphan\n```\nThen also collect files in the 7 dirs? No, those aren't orphans. So only walk non-7 top-level dirs + top-level files.\n\nSimplest: walk entire test dir skipping fixtures/helpers/perf subtrees, collect all *.test.mjs as `allTestFiles`; compute enumerated set; orphans = allTestFiles - enumerated. That elegantly handles everything: files in the 7 dirs are enumerated → not orphans; files elsewhere → orphans.\n\n`test/select.test.mjs` would be an orphan → that's why it must move.\n\n### Filtered run file selection\n\"在枚举结果里选出文本中含字面量 `[AC-nnn]` 的文件\" — read file content and check contains `[AC-074]`. Since AC-074 test file contains the string. Good.\n\nA1-03 expects only `test/unit/milestone-gate.test.mjs` for --ac AC-074. So no other file may contain \"[AC-074]\". Careful: my fixtures might contain [AC-074] strings... Fixtures excluded. `test/traceability/T-001.json` is not a .test.mjs. But `test/unit/traceability.test.mjs` — does it contain '[AC-074]'? I must avoid writing '[AC-074]' literally in traceability.test.mjs. Also runner-selftest.test.mjs must not contain '[AC-074]'. And a3-runner.test.mjs must not.\n\nSimilarly A1-10 expects `--ac AC-001` → runner-selftest.test.mjs included (just includes, not equality). Fine.\n\nA4-07 expects `--ac AC-004` → files join = `test/unit/traceability.test.mjs` exactly. So no other file may contain '[AC-004]'. Careful: runner-selftest might mention AC-004 in text; avoid. Also milestone-gate.test.mjs must not contain '[AC-004]'.\n\nA1-06 expects filtered run on fixture ok with AC-001 → only `test/unit/a.test.mjs`. So fixture test/integration/b.test.mjs and flows/c.test.mjs must not contain '[AC-001]'. Design says only a.test.mjs has [AC-001]. OK.\n\n### Manifest written even when subprocess fails\nNode writes the reporter output file. If the subprocess crashes hard, file might not exist. We handle: if read fails, treat manifest as {mode:...}? For full run, missing manifest → mode_not_full at least. I'll implement: try read; on error use `{ mode: null, files: [] }` so verifyFullRun reports mode_not_full plus missing for each enumerated. Then exit with child code (nonzero) anyway.\n\n### Exit codes\n- child code null → 1.\n\n### printing\n`run-tests: <mode> <n> files, manifest <path>` at end. A1-01 checks `run-tests: full`.\n\n### --perf\nOnly enumerate test/perf/**/*.test.mjs, run them, no manifest. If none → print `run-tests: no perf tests`, exit 0. Should not create out dir? \"不写 files-executed.json\". A1-12 expects output contains 'no perf tests'. I'll skip creating out dir entirely in perf mode... but §3.3 step 1 says create dir, delete old manifest, truncate skips. For perf, simpler: if no perf files, print and exit 0 without spawning. If there are files, run them without reporter (just `--test <files>`). Since test/perf doesn't exist now, fine.\n\nHmm: A1-12 says \"该命令不得写 files-executed.json\". If I truncate skips.jsonl in the default tmp dir that's fine (not repo). I'll early-return before touching dir for perf when empty. Better: for perf mode, don't touch outDir at all.\n\n### `check-test-discovery.mjs`\nModes:\n- no args → full selftest\n- --root [--manifest]\n\nSelftest steps per §9. Need mkdtemp dirs and spawn run-tests on fixtures.\n\nStep 2: run `run-tests.mjs --root test/fixtures/discovery-selftest/ok --out-dir <tmp>`; exit 0; manifest mode full; files exactly expected 3.\n\nStep 3: verifyFullRun on drift manifests: read the 4 manifests from drift dir, enumerated = enumerateTestFiles(drift). Compare.\n\nStep 4: findOrphans(orphan) includes 'test/loose.test.mjs'.\n\nStep 5: run filtered on ok with --ac AC-001 → mode filtered, files length 1.\n\nOutputs `check-test-discovery: ok`.\n\nNote: `--root` for the ok fixture is relative to cwd. run-tests resolves root via path.resolve. Then relative paths in manifest are relative to that root. Good.\n\nBut careful: run-tests sets cwd to <root> for the child. The fixture tests import only node:assert and node:test — no relative imports. Good.\n\nAlso decoys under ok/test/fixtures etc. contain assert.fail. They won't be enumerated.\n\nAlso: findOrphans(ok fixture) must be empty — decoy files are under fixtures/helpers/perf, excluded. ✓\n\nImportant: The `ok` fixture has `test/unit/a.test.mjs` with title `[AC-001] ...`. For A1-06 filtered run, the file must contain literal '[AC-001]'. ✓\n\n### check-fast\nFiles in `<dir>` matching ^check-.*\\.mjs$, excluding check-fast.mjs, sorted by code point. Then final step: `process.execPath <dir>/run-tests.mjs`.\n\nWait — in A3-01 injection dir f contains check-a, check-b, check-c and final.mjs. Files matching ^check-.*\\.mjs$: check-a, check-b, check-c (final.mjs doesn't match). Sorted → a,b,c. Final script from CLIWORKER_TEST_FINAL_SCRIPT.\n\nExpected order a,b then stop at b (exit 7), log = a,b. ✓\n\nA3-03: after modifying check-b to succeed, adds a `check-fast.mjs` to the injection dir which must NOT be executed. Our exclusion is by filename === 'check-fast.mjs' → excluded. ✓ Log = a,b,c,final. ✓\n\nPrint to stdout `==> <文件名>` for each step and `==> npm test` for final. In A3-01 the failing step name must appear in output; `$r.Out -match 'check-b\\.mjs'` — stdout is captured, and stderr message `check-fast: FAILED step check-b.mjs (exit 7)`. Either works.\n\nReal run A3-06: steps sorted alphabetically: check-ci.mjs, check-defects.mjs, check-test-discovery.mjs, check-traceability.mjs. Note check-defects sorts before check-test-discovery (\"d\" < \"t\"). Expected order in A3-06: check-ci, check-defects, check-test-discovery, check-traceability. ✓ Then `==> npm test`. And must not print `==> check-fast.mjs`. ✓\n\n`<dir>` default = dirname of check-fast.mjs. Child cwd = parent of that dir = repo root.\n\nInjection env: if either var used, print to stderr `check-fast: test injection active`.\n\nWait: A3-06 runs the real `scripts/check-fast.ps1` and `npm run check` with no injection. Fine.\n\nBut careful: in A3-01/A3-02, CLIWORKER_TEST_CHECK_DIR = f (temp), so dir = f; final script = f/final.mjs; cwd = parent of f (temp dir's parent). Fine.\n\nReal npm test inside check-fast: run-tests with default root → repo root. Its child cwd = repo root. But wait — `npm test` equivalent is `process.execPath <dir>/run-tests.mjs` where dir = scripts. cwd = repo root. Good.\n\n### check-ci / gen-ci\nrenderCi(matrix) produces exact YAML. check-ci compares with file bytes.\n\nMust be careful that the generated file passes A2-03:\n- contains 'continue-on-error: true' exactly once → only perf job.\n- contains 'needs:' 0 times.\n- contains 'npm run check', 'actions/upload-artifact@v4', 'ubuntu-latest','windows-latest','macos-latest','files-executed.json','skips.jsonl','CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR'.\n\nAlso A2-04 checks stale detection via appending '# x'.\n\nMatrix validation first (ci.matrix_invalid), then missing ci.yml, then stale.\n\nA2-04 flow:\n1. copy matrix + ci.yml into temp root → clean: 0. Note the temp copy of ci.yml must equal renderCi(parsed matrix). Since we generate from the same matrix, yes. But when the temp matrix is rewritten by ConvertTo-Json (blocking:true), matrix invalid fires before compare. Good.\n2. Append '# x' → stale: 1.\n3. Restore ci.yml, set perf.blocking=true → matrix_invalid: 1. Note: the rewritten matrix via ConvertTo-Json has different formatting but same content; our validation of matrix fields must not depend on formatting. ✓\n4. Set node=[20] → matrix_invalid.\n\nNote `ConvertTo-Json` on a PSCustomObject: node becomes [20] array → after ConvertTo-Json, `\"node\": [20]`? Actually `$m.node = @(20)` then serialization gives `\"node\": [20]`. Our check: `matrix.node` must be exactly [20,22] → fail. ✓\n\nCareful: `check_command`, `artifacts`, `perf.blocking`, `os` names set. Also maybe check os runs_on/baseline? Design says only name set equality. I'll check names set equals exactly {ubuntu,windows,macos} (sorted equality).\n\nrenderCi must be deterministic; and must produce exactly what's committed.\n\nAlso note: check-ci must read matrix as JSON manually (fs) not require (cache). Use fs.readFileSync + JSON.parse.\n\nYAML content design:\n\n```yaml\n# generated by scripts/gen-ci.mjs from scripts/ci-matrix.json - do not edit\nname: ci\non: [push, pull_request]\npermissions:\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  check:\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        include:\n          - name: ubuntu\n            runs_on: ubuntu-latest\n            node: 20\n          ...\n    runs-on: ${{ matrix.runs_on }}\n    env:\n      CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR: ${{ github.workspace }}/test-results\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: ${{ matrix.node }}\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run check\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n        if: always()\n        with:\n          name: test-results-${{ matrix.name }}-node${{ matrix.node }}\n          path: |\n            test-results/files-executed.json\n            test-results/skips.jsonl\n          if-no-files-found: warn\n  perf:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    continue-on-error: true\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: 22\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run perf\n```\n\nCareful: `name: test-results-${{ matrix.name }}-node${{ matrix.node }}` — contains `${{`. Fine in a template string if I escape `${`? In JS template literals, `${{` would be interpreted as `${` followed by `{`. I must escape with `\\${` or use string concatenation / plain quotes. I'll build with array join and use `'${{ ... }}'` in single-quoted strings where possible.\n\nAlso note `on: [push, pull_request]` — YAML 1.1 treats `on` as boolean in some parsers but not relevant.\n\nDoes the perf job need `needs`? No. And nothing `needs` it. `needs:` count must be 0. ✓\n\nNow `continue-on-error: true` appears once. ✓\n\nBut careful: is `strategy.fail-fast: false` also fine.\n\nA2-03 expects `ubuntu-latest` etc. count ≥1 — present.\n\n`check-ci` stale check: compare exact string. My generator must end with trailing newline. The committed file must be byte-identical. I'll write renderCi output with '\\n' endings and final newline.\n\n### check-traceability\nBiggest piece. Let's write carefully.\n\nExports: validateIndex(index), mergeShards({root, index}), extractTitles(source). Plus CLI.\n\nvalidateIndex(index) → errors[] of {id, ac?, detail}:\n- index.schema: schema !==