here. Careful in comments. Also test files under test/unit/*.test.mjs must not contain '.collab' (A4-08 scans test/unit/*.test.mjs and test/helpers). And scripts/*.mjs and scripts/reporters/*.mjs.\n\nA4-08 scans: `scripts/*.mjs`, `scripts/reporters/*.mjs`, `test/helpers/platform-gate.mjs`, `test/unit/*.test.mjs`. Must find 0 occurrences of '.collab'. So in traceability.test.mjs, when testing --task-graph I must pass a path constructed without the literal: e.g. `path.join('..', '..', '.collab', ...)` — that contains '.collab' literally! Must avoid: build as `path.join('..','..', '.' + 'collab', 'blueprint', ...)`. Or just pass a synthetic task-graph fixture... but write_scope has no fixtures for task graph except test/fixtures/traceability/** — allowed! I can create `test/fixtures/traceability/task-graph.json`. But it's not in design's fixture list. Hmm, design says fixtures are those; adding an extra file within test/fixtures/traceability/** is allowed by write_scope. But design says \"夹具覆盖...\" listing specific dirs. Adding a task-graph fixture might conflict with check-traceability reading `test/fixtures/traceability/*.json`? No, that's `<root>/test/traceability`.\n\nHowever A4-05 passes `--root \"$b/$c[0]\"` which are subdirs; a file at test/fixtures/traceability/task-graph.json is not read. Safe.\n\nBut careful: does test/fixtures/traceability/ac-index.json get scanned by findOrphans? No, it's not .test.mjs and fixtures are excluded.\n\nSimplest for A4-02 verification: runner-selftest doesn't need to test task-graph. But A4-07 asserts specific strings in traceability.test.mjs: index.count, index.owner, index.milestone, index.levels, index.baseline, index.baseline_incompatible, index.schema, shard.missing_title. graph.mismatch not required. I'll still test task-graph with a synthetic fixture to be thorough, using a path without '.collab'. I'll create test/fixtures/traceability/task-graph.json containing a mini task graph with one task accepting AC-001..003, AC-004, AC-074 mismatching deliberately? Actually the fixture must have real-ish data to test mismatch detection. I'll make a fixture with one task T-001 M1 accepting AC-001 (levels unit) and AC-004 — and assert mismatch when index says AC-001 owner T-002? Hmm, need index alignment.\n\nSimpler: test `--task-graph` against the fixture index (74 entries) using a fixture task-graph that lists AC-001 owned by T-999 → expect graph.mismatch. I'll generate a task-graph fixture programmatically... but it must be a static file in write_scope: test/fixtures/traceability/task-graph.json. I can write it with just a couple tasks. The comparison only iterates over index entries and looks up acceptance; missing owner → need to decide: if no task lists the AC → graph.mismatch (\"owner not found\"). That would produce 74 mismatches. For a focused test, use `--index` pointing to a small custom index... but ac-index fixtures are fixed.\n\nAlternative: skip deep task-graph test; A4-02 covers the real one. But then graph.mismatch untested — acceptable? Design says `--task-graph` is optional cross-check. Tests required: §10 lists traceability tests as \"§6.4 的夹具用例、validateIndex 的内存变异、真实 ac-index.json 通过校验、extractTitles 对 test.skip 的处理\". No task-graph test required. \n\nI'll add a minimal synthetic task-graph test using an index fixture subset: create `test/fixtures/traceability/mismatch/task-graph.json` + reuse a small index file? The check requires index and shards. I could create `test/fixtures/traceability/graph/` with its own `ac-index.json`... but check-traceability CLI takes `--index`. Actually I can pass `--index test/fixtures/traceability/ac-index.json` (74 entries) and a task-graph fixture containing tasks covering all 74 correctly except one → 1 mismatch. That's a big fixture to hand-write.\n\nDecision: generate the real cross-check in acceptance (A4-02 passes the real path), and in unit tests just assert that a task-graph file with a deliberately wrong owner for AC-001 yields graph.mismatch, constructing the task-graph JSON in memory and writing to mkdtemp at test time (not a repo fixture). Writing to mkdtemp is fine — files outside repo. Yes! Tests can create temp files. I'll build a task graph object derived from the real index but with AC-001 owner wrong, write to mkdtemp, pass `--task-graph`. But then --root must be repo root and shards from repo... that's fine, exit will be 1 due to graph.mismatch but also shard errors? Repo shards are valid. Good. But careful: the test would depend on the repo root having contracts/spec/ac-index.json. Fine.\n\nHmm, but building a task graph from the index requires a mapping ac→owner/milestone which the test can derive from the index itself (read ac-index). That's fine and doesn't contain '.collab'.\n\nOK. Let me not over-engineer; I'll implement graph check and test it with a temp file.\n\nmergeShards({root, index}) → { errors, acs }:\n- shardDir = <root>/test/traceability\n- if missing → no shards, all planned.\n- for each *.json sorted:\n  - parse; on error → shard.schema\n  - task must equal basename without .json → shard.schema\n  - must have `entries` array; each entry: keys ac, status, files (design: \"条目缺 ac/status/files\" → shard.schema). Extra keys allowed? Except `baseline` forbidden. Design says shard.baseline_forbidden if 分片顶层或任一条目带 baseline. Other extra keys — not specified; allow (fixture T-001.json has task, milestone, entries: milestone is extra top-level key, allowed).\n  - status must be covered|planned else shard.schema\n  - ac not in index → shard.unknown_ac\n  - duplicate: same AC twice in one shard or across shards → shard.duplicate\n  - shard.task !== index owner → shard.not_owner\n  - covered: files empty → shard.missing_file; any file missing or absolute or contains '..' → shard.missing_file\n  - covered: no file containing a plain-kind title containing `[AC-nnn]` → shard.missing_title\n\nNote ordering: check duplicates before not_owner? For duplicate fixture: T-001 and T-002 both list AC-001. A4-06 expects errors ids unique set = shard.duplicate,shard.not_owner. So both must be reported. Since T-002 lists AC-001 which is owned by T-001 → not_owner. And duplicate across shards. Both. ✓\n\nFor missing-title fixture: file exists with only [AC-002] → missing_title. Also would missing_title be accompanied by others? No, single shard T-001 with AC-001 covered.\n\nFor `ok/` fixture: T-001 with AC-001 covered (file exists with test('[AC-001] ...')), AC-002 planned. Then AC-001's owner must be T-001 ✓ and file exists relative to root.\n\nStatuses map: acs = Map/object ac→status, default planned.\n\nCovered count in output: count of 'covered'.\n\n`acs` in JSON output: `acs:[{ac,status}]` — for all 74? Design: `--json` outputs `{ ok, errors, covered, planned, acs:[{ac,status}] }`. A4-06 only checks ok and error ids. I'll include all index ACs sorted.\n\nextractTitles(source): regex given:\n`/(?<![\\w.])(test|it|describe|platformTest)(\\.(skip|todo|only))?\\s*\\(\\s*(['\"`])((?:\\\\.|(?!\\4).)*)\\4/g`\n\nNote `\\4` backreference to group 4 (the quote). Group 3 is the quote. Wait groups: 1=(test|it|describe|platformTest), 2=.(skip|todo|only), 3=(skip|todo|only), 4=quote. Then `\\4` refers to quote. In my implementation I must keep group numbering: use non-capturing for inner? The design regex has `(\\.(skip|todo|only))?` with capturing groups. If I copy exactly, group 4 is the quote. I'll copy exactly and use match[1], match[3] (kind), match[4] (quote), match[5] (title). Careful: `((?:\\\\.|(?!\\4).)*)` is group 5.\n\nSo:\n```js\nconst TITLE_RE = /(?<![\\w.])(test|it|describe|platformTest)(\\.(skip|todo|only))?\\s*\\(\\s*(['\"`])((?:\\\\.|(?!\\4).)*)\\4/g\n```\nkind = m[3] ? m[3] : 'plain'.\n\nNote lookbehind `(?<![\\w.])` — supported in Node 20. ✓\n\nHandle unescaping? Titles like `'[AC-001] ...'` — no escapes. I'll use the raw group content (may include backslash escapes). Acceptable; but for `[AC-001]` match, content contains it literally.\n\nAlso