lestone\": \"M1\", \"levels\": [\"unit\"], \"baseline\": [\"darwin\", \"linux\", \"win32\"] },\n```\n74 lines + wrapper. That's clean, valid, 2-space indent at the array level. Design says \"每条的键顺序固定为 ac, owner, milestone, levels, baseline\" — preserved. I'll do that. Key order in the object is as written.\n\nHmm, \"2 空格缩进\" I'll honor for structural nesting: root `{`, `  \"schema\"`, `  \"acs\": [`, `    { ... }`, `  ]`, `}`.\n\nThat's defensible and much more maintainable.\n\nNow write it.\n\nBaseline default `[\"darwin\", \"linux\", \"win32\"]`.\n\nLet me write the file.\n\nThen the checker must validate key order? Design says key order fixed, but validation only checks \"条目含有五个键之外的键、缺键\". I'll also not enforce order (A4-04 checks `Object.keys(x.acs[0])` = exact order — that's data, fine).\n\nNow let me write all files. This is a big task; let me be systematic.\n\nLet me start with ac-index.json.\n\nFor AC-038: levels [\"smoke\"], baseline [\"real-smoke\"].\n\nNow, writing.\n\nAlso `test/fixtures/traceability/ac-index.json` — identical content.\n\nLet me now think about run-tests.mjs implementation in full detail.\n\n```js\n#!/usr/bin/env node\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nimport os from 'node:os'\nimport path from 'node:path'\nimport crypto from 'node:crypto'\nimport { spawn } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url'\n\nconst HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\nconst REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..')\n\nconst TOP_DIRS = ['unit','integration','contract','e2e','smoke','characterization','flows']\nconst EXCLUDED = new Set(['fixtures','helpers','perf'])\n\nfunction toPosix(p) { return p.split(path.sep).join('/') }\n\nexport function enumerateTestFiles(root) {\n  const base = path.resolve(root)\n  const out = []\n  for (const dir of TOP_DIRS) {\n    const start = path.join(base, 'test', dir)\n    if (!fs.existsSync(start)) continue\n    walk(start, (file) => { if (file.endsWith('.test.mjs')) out.push(toPosix(path.relative(base, file))) })\n  }\n  return [...new Set(out)].sort()\n}\n```\nwalk: recursive readdirSync with withFileTypes.\n\nActually if `start` is a file not dir, skip.\n\nfindOrphans:\n```js\nexport function findOrphans(root) {\n  const base = path.resolve(root)\n  const testDir = path.join(base, 'test')\n  if (!fs.existsSync(testDir)) return []\n  const all = []\n  walkTest(testDir, all)  // skip dirs in EXCLUDED at any level? Only at top-level of test dir per spec, but skipping any dir named fixtures/helpers/perf would also skip test/unit/fixtures which might contain .test.mjs — those wouldn't be enumerated either so they'd be \"orphans\" if counted. Better: only skip at testDir root level, matching \"test/fixtures 整个排除\".\n```\nHmm. If a file at test/unit/fixtures/x.test.mjs exists, it's not enumerated (enumeration only under 7 dirs recursively — actually it IS under unit recursively, so it would be enumerated!). Wait, enumerateTestFiles walks test/unit recursively and collects all *.test.mjs, including test/unit/fixtures/x.test.mjs. So no conflict.\n\nFor findOrphans, I want: all *.test.mjs under test/ except those under test/fixtures, test/helpers, test/perf subtrees, minus enumerated set. Directories named fixtures inside unit would be enumerated, so not orphans. So skipping only the three top-level dirs is right.\n\n```js\nfunction walk(dir, onFile, skipNames) {\n  let entries\n  try { entries = fs.readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true }) } catch { return }\n  for (const e of entries) {\n    const full = path.join(dir, e.name)\n    if (e.isDirectory()) { if (skipNames && skipNames.has(e.name)) continue; walk(full, onFile, skipNames) }\n    else if (e.isFile()) onFile(full)\n  }\n}\n```\nCareful: skipNames applies at every level then. For findOrphans I want skip only at top level. I'll pass skipNames only for the first call then null for recursion. Simpler: handle top-level explicitly.\n\n```js\nexport function findOrphans(root) {\n  const base = path.resolve(root)\n  const testDir = path.join(base, 'test')\n  if (!fs.existsSync(testDir)) return []\n  const all = []\n  for (const e of fs.readdirSync(testDir, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n    if (e.isDirectory()) {\n      if (EXCLUDED.has(e.name)) continue\n      walk(path.join(testDir, e.name), (f) => { if (f.endsWith('.test.mjs')) all.push(f) })\n    } else if (e.isFile() && e.name.endsWith('.test.mjs')) all.push(path.join(testDir, e.name))\n  }\n  const enumerated = new Set(enumerateTestFiles(base))\n  return all.map(f => toPosix(path.relative(base, f))).filter(f => !enumerated.has(f)).sort()\n}\n```\nDedup not needed but fine.\n\nverifyFullRun:\n```js\nexport function verifyFullRun({ enumerated, manifest }) {\n  const errors = []\n  const files = Array.isArray(manifest?.files) ? manifest.files : []\n  if (!manifest || manifest.mode !== 'full') errors.push({ id: 'discovery.mode_not_full', path: '' })\n  const set = new Set(files)\n  for (const f of enumerated) if (!set.has(f)) errors.push({ id: 'discovery.missing', path: f })\n  const en = new Set(enumerated)\n  for (const f of files) if (!en.has(f)) errors.push({ id: 'discovery.unexpected', path: f })\n  if (enumerated.length === 0) errors.push({ id: 'discovery.no_files', path: '' })\n  return { ok: errors.length === 0, errors }\n}\n```\nOrder: mode first. For manifest-ok (full, equal) → ok.\n\nPath format in FAIL line: `FAIL <id> <path>` — when path empty, trailing space. Acceptable. A1-09 only matches id pattern.\n\nCLI parse:\n```js\nfunction usage() { console.error('usage: node scripts/run-tests.mjs [--ac AC-nnn]... [--root <dir>] [--out-dir <dir>] [--perf] [--update-snapshots]') }\n```\nparse loop with index.\n\n`--ac` value: must match /^AC-\\d{3}$/ else exit 2.\n\noutDir resolution:\n```js\nfunction defaultOutDir(root) {\n  const key = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(process.platform === 'win32' ? root.toLowerCase() : root).digest('hex').slice(0,12)\n  return path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'cliworker-test-results', key)\n}\n```\nNote root should be absolute for hashing: use path.resolve(root).\n\nguard:\n```js\nconst activeEnv = process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE\nif (activeEnv && process.env.CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR && samePath(outDir, process.env.CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR)) { console.error(...); process.exit(2) }\n```\nCareful: A1-11 sets CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR=$o and --out-dir $o. samePath compares resolved, case-insensitive on win32, and strip trailing separators.\n\nBut hold on: is this guard also triggered during `npm test` when the test itself calls `run-tests` with `--out-dir <mkdtemp>`? No, different.\n\nBut there's a subtle problem: in `npm test`, CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE=1 and CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR=<d>. In runner-selftest we spawn run-tests with `--out-dir $o` where $o is a fresh mkdtemp → differs. ✓\n\nPerf mode:\n```js\nif (perf) {\n  const files = enumeratePerf(root)\n  if (files.length === 0) { console.log('run-tests: no perf tests'); process.exit(0) }\n  spawn node --test ... files, stdio inherit; exit code\n}\n```\nenumeratePerf: walk <root>/test/perf for *.test.mjs (if exists). Note test/perf is excluded from normal enumeration.\n\nFull/filtered flow:\n1. compute enumerated\n2. if full: orphans = findOrphans(root); if any → print `FAIL discovery.orphan <p>` and exit 1.\n   Note: also for filtered? Design says only full checks orphans (\"完整运行：...同时 findOrphans\"). I'll check orphans only in full mode. Hmm A1-07 uses full run on orphan fixture → exit 1 ✓.\n3. filtered: select files whose content includes '[AC-nnn]'.\n   For multiple --ac, union. Check each ac literal.\n   If none → `FAIL run.no_match <AC>` and exit 1. With multiple ACs, print each? Design says `<AC>`. I'll print one line per AC with no match? Simpler: `FAIL run.no_match ${acs.join(',')}`. A1-04 matches 'run.no_match'. I'll print `FAIL run.no_match AC-999`.\n   Exit 1.\n4. prepare outDir: mkdir -p; rm files-executed.json; write skips.jsonl '' (if not exists → create; if exists → truncate).\n   Design: