{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b64e-af3f-7273-ac32-c8e9050d56b1","session_id":"01a1b64b-fe4d-73dd-9fb2-26f680d0b3a8","message":{"id":"01a1b64eaf3f7273ac32c8e7b3a093f1","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now scripts/check-traceability.mjs. Let me write it carefully.\n\nKey details:\n- No '.collab' literal.\n- Exports validateIndex, mergeShards, extractTitles.\n- CLI.\n\nDefault repo root = dirname(dirname(script)).\n\nLet me write.\n\n```js\n#!/usr/bin/env node\nimport fs from 'node:fs'\nimport path from 'node:path'\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'\n\nconst HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url))\nconst REPO_ROOT = path.resolve(HERE, '..')\n\nconst SCHEMA = 'cliworker.ac-index/v1'\nconst LEVELS = new Set(['unit', 'integration', 'contract', 'e2e', 'smoke'])\nconst BASELINES = new Set(['linux', 'win32', 'darwin', 'real-smoke'])\nconst MILESTONES = new Set(['M1','M2','M3','M4','M5'])\nconst INDEX_KEYS = ['ac','owner','milestone','levels','baseline']\nconst NON_SMOKE_LEVELS = ['unit','contract','integration','e2e']\n```\n\nexpectedAcs(): Array.from({length:74},(_,i)=>`AC-${String(i+1).padStart(3,'0')}`)\n\nvalidateIndex(index):\n```js\nexport function validateIndex(index) {\n  const errors = []\n  const push = (id, ac, detail) => errors.push({ id, ac, detail })\n  if (!index || typeof index !== 'object' || Array.isArray(index)) { push('index.schema', undefined, 'index must be an object'); return errors }\n  if (index.schema !== SCHEMA) push('index.schema', undefined, `schema must be ${SCHEMA}`)\n  const acs = Array.isArray(index.acs) ? index.acs : null\n  if (!acs) { push('index.schema', undefined, 'acs must be an array'); return errors }\n  const expected = expectedAcs()\n  const seen = []\n  for (const entry of acs) {\n    if (!entry || typeof entry !== 'object' || Array.isArray(entry)) { push('index.schema', undefined, 'entry must be an object'); continue }\n    const keys = Object.keys(entry)\n    const missing = INDEX_KEYS.filter((k) => !keys.includes(k))\n    const extra = keys.filter((k) => !INDEX_KEYS.includes(k))\n    if (missing.length || extra.length) push('index.schema', entry.ac, `entry keys must be exactly ${INDEX_KEYS.join(',')} (missing=${missing.join('|')||'-'} extra=${extra.join('|')||'-'})`)\n    ...\n  }\n```\nCareful: continue after missing keys? If `ac` missing we can't proceed much. I'll guard each field type.\n\nThen count check:\n```js\n  const got = acs.map(a => a && a.ac)\n  const same = got.length === expected.length && got.every((a,i)=>a===expected[i])\n  if (!same) {\n    const duplicates = got.filter((a,i)=>got.indexOf(a)!==i)\n    push('index.count', undefined, `expected exactly ${expected[0]}..${expected[73]} in order (got ${got.length} entries${duplicates.length?`, duplicates: ${[...new Set(duplicates)].join(',')}`:''})`)\n  }\n```\n\nPer-entry validation:\n- owner: typeof string && /^T-\\d{3}$/\n- milestone in MILESTONES\n- levels: array, non-empty, every in LEVELS, no dups\n- baseline similar\n- baseline_incompatible.\n\nOrder of errors matters for tests? Tests just include id.\n\nmergeShards({root, index}):\n```js\nexport function mergeShards({ root, index }) {\n  const errors = []\n  const acs = new Map()\n  const ownerOf = new Map()\n  for (const e of index?.acs ?? []) ownerOf.set(e.ac, e.owner)\n  const dir = path.join(root, 'test', 'traceability')\n  let names = []\n  try { names = fs.readdirSync(dir).filter(n => n.endsWith('.json')).sort() } catch { names = [] }\n  const shardAcCount = new Map()\n  for (const name of names) {\n    const file = path.join(dir, name)\n    let shard\n    try { shard = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(file, 'utf8')) } catch (err) { errors.push({id:'shard.schema', file: `test/traceability/${name}`, detail: 'invalid JSON'}); continue }\n    if (!shard || typeof shard !== 'object' || Array.isArray(shard)) { errors.push({id:'shard.schema', file: ..., detail:'shard must be an object'}); continue }\n    const task = name.replace(/\\.json$/, '')\n    if (typeof shard.task !== 'string' || shard.task !== task) errors.push({id:'shard.schema', file, detail:`task must be \"${task}\"`})\n    if ('baseline' in shard) errors.push({id:'shard.baseline_forbidden', file, detail:'shard must not declare baseline'})\n    const entries = Array.isArray(shard.entries) ? shard.entries : null\n    if (!entries) { errors.push({id:'shard.schema', file, detail:'entries must be an array'}); continue }\n    const inShard = new Map()\n    for (const entry of entries) {\n      if (!entry || typeof entry !== 'object') { errors.push({id:'shard.schema',...}); continue }\n      if ('baseline' in entry) errors.push({id:'shard.baseline_forbidden', file, ac: entry.ac, detail:'entry must not declare baseline'})\n      const { ac, status, files } = entry\n      if (typeof ac !== 'string' || ac === '' || (status !== 'covered' && status !== 'planned') || !Array.isArray(files)) {\n        errors.push({id:'shard.schema', file, ac, detail:'entry needs ac, status(covered|planned), files'}); continue\n      }\n      ...\n```\nHmm: \"条目缺 ac/status/files\" — if files missing entirely (undefined) it's shard.schema, not missing_file. Design distinguishes: `shard.missing_file` = \"covered 条目的 files 为空\". So `files` must be present and an array; empty array → missing_file for covered.\n\nThen:\n```js\n      if (!ownerOf.has(ac)) { errors.push({id:'shard.unknown_ac', ac, file, detail:'not in index'}); continue }\n      if (inShard.has(ac) || shardAcCount.has(ac)) { errors.push({id:'shard.duplicate', ac, file, detail: inShard.has(ac) ? 'duplicate within shard' : `already declared in ${shardAcCount.get(ac)}`}) }\n      else { inShard.set(ac, status); shardAcCount.set(ac, `test/traceability/${name}`) }\n```\nWait duplicate across shards: if AC already in shardAcCount from a previous shard → duplicate. Set status: first wins. \n\nBut careful: the duplicate fixture expects both duplicate and not_owner. Since T-002 lists AC-001 → not_owner also fires.\n\n```js\n      if (shard.task !== ownerOf.get(ac)) errors.push({id:'shard.not_owner', ac, file, detail:`owner is ${ownerOf.get(ac)}`})\n```\nHmm, if shard.task is invalid, we still compare — fine.\n\nThen for covered:\n```js\n      if (status === 'covered') {\n        let bad = false\n        if (files.length === 0) { errors.push({id:'shard.missing_file', ac, file, detail:'covered entry needs at least one file'}); bad = true }\n        for (const f of files) {\n          if (typeof f !== 'string' || f === '' || path.isAbsolute(f) || f.split('/').includes('..') || f.split('\\\\').includes('..')) { errors.push({id:'shard.missing_file', ac, file, detail:`bad path ${f}`}); bad = true; continue }\n          if (!fs.existsSync(path.join(root, f))) { errors.push({id:'shard.missing_file', ac, file, detail:`missing ${f}`}); bad = true }\n        }\n        if (!bad) {\n          let has = false\n          for (const f of files) {\n            let src = ''\n            try { src = fs.readFileSync(path.join(root, f), 'utf8') } catch { continue }\n            if (extractTitles(src).some(t => t.kind === 'plain' && t.title.includes(`[${ac}]`))) { has = true; break }\n          }\n          if (!has) errors.push({id:'shard.missing_title', ac, file, detail:`no plain test title contains [${ac}]`})\n        }\n      }\n```\nThen status recording: for explicit planned entries, status planned.\n\nFinally compute acs map over index entries (default planned):\n```js\n  const statuses = {}\n  for (const e of index?.acs ?? []) statuses[e.ac] = 'planned'\n  for (const [ac, st] of shardAcCount.entries()) ...\n```\nHmm I need status map keyed by ac. Let me keep `declared = new Map()` ac→status (first occurrence).\n\nAt the end:\n```js\n  const acs = {}\n  for (const e of index?.acs ?? []) acs[e.ac] = declared.has(e.ac) ? declared.get(e.ac) : 'planned'\n```\nDesign says mergeShards returns `{ errors, acs }` where acs is `ac → 'covered'|'planned'`. I'll return a plain object.\n\nNote: if the index is invalid/empty, acs = {}.\n\nAlso note: duplicate within a single shard — same ac twice in one shard's entries. My inShard check handles it.\n\nBut careful: `shardAcCount` used to detect across-shard duplicate; I set it when first declared. Let me use one map `declared` for both: if declared.has(ac) → duplicate (message notes whether same shard).\n\nLet me restructure:\n```js\nconst declared = new Map() // ac -> {status, shard}\n```\nIn entry loop:\n```js\nif (declared.has(ac)) {\n  const prev = declared.get(ac)\n  errors.push({id:'shard.duplicate', ac, file, detail: prev.shard === rel ? 'duplicate within shard' : `already declared in ${prev.shard}`})\n} else {\n  declared.set(ac, { status, shard: rel })\n}\n```\nBut then for duplicate entries we still validate files etc.? In the duplicate fixture, T-002's AC-001 covered with files ['test/unit/gate.test.mjs']? I'll make T-002's entry covered with a valid file to keep it simple; then it may also produce not_owner only. Good.\n\nCareful: if duplicate entry has invalid files, extra errors appear. Acceptable (test only checks presence).\n\nA4-06 expects error ids sorted unique = exactly `shard.duplicate,shard.not_owner`. So the duplicate fixture must NOT produce other errors. So both shards' AC-001 entries must be covered with valid files and titles. T-001 owns AC-001; T-002 must produce not_owner. Files must exist with [AC-001] title. I'll point both to the same file `test/unit/gate.test.mjs` in the duplicate fixture. But careful: duplicate check `declared` — second declaration still runs file checks; both valid → no extra errors. ✓\n\nAlso the duplicate fixture must have no other ACs. And shards T-001.json, T-002.json. T-002's task T-002. Since T-002 doesn't own AC-001 → not_owner once. ✓\n\nNow the CLI.\n\n```js\nfunction main() {\n  const args = process.argv.slice(2)\n  let root = REPO_ROOT, indexPath = null, taskGraph = null, json = false\n  for (let i=0;i<args.length;i++) {\n    const a = args[i]\n    if (a === '--root') { root = path.resolve(args[++i] ?? '') }\n    else if (a === '--index') { indexPath = path.resolve(args[++i] ?? '') }\n    else if (a === '--task-graph') { taskGraph = path.resolve(args[++i] ?? '') }\n    else if (a === '--json') json = true\n    else { console.error('...usage'); process.exit(2) }\n  }\n```\nHmm, missing value handling: `args[++i]` undefined → path.resolve(undefined) throws. Guard with a helper `need()`.\n\nDefault index = path.join(root, 'contracts/spec/ac-index.json').\n\nLoad index; if read/parse fails → error index.schema with detail.\n\nThen validateIndex. Then mergeShards. Then optional graph check.\n\nNote design: should merge still run if index invalid? Probably yes but results meaningless. I'll run validate first; if index parse failed, skip merge and report only schema error. If index has errors but parses, still merge (it's fine).\n\nHmm — careful with fixture A4-05: `--index test/fixtures/traceability/ac-index.json` valid → no index errors. ✓\n\nGraph check:\n```js\nif (taskGraph) {\n  let g\n  try { g = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(taskGraph,'utf8')) } catch { errors.push({id:'graph.mismatch', detail:'task graph unreadable'}) }\n  if (g) {\n    const owner = new Map(), milestone = new Map(), levels = new Map()\n    for (const t of g.tasks ?? []) for (const ac of t.acceptance ?? []) { owner.set(ac, t.id); milestone.set(ac, t.milestone); levels.set(ac, Array.isArray(t.levels)? t.levels : null) }\n```\nHmm — task-graph tasks don't have `levels` per task; levels are in the acceptance array entries (`acceptance[].levels`). So:\n```js\n    const graphLevels = new Map()\n    for (const a of g.acceptance ?? []) graphLevels.set(a.id, a.levels)\n```\nand owner from tasks' acceptance lists, milestone from task.milestone.\n\nThen for each index entry:\n- if owner.get(ac) !== entry.owner → graph.mismatch\n- if milestone.get(ac) !== entry.milestone → graph.mismatch\n- if graphLevels.has(ac) and set(levels) != set(graphLevels) → graph.mismatch\n\nGood — A4-02 must produce no mismatch for the real graph.\n\nOutput:\n```js\n  if (json) {\n    const out = { ok: errors.length===0, errors: errors.map(e=>({id:e.id, ...(e.ac?{ac:e.ac}:{}), ...(e.file?{file:e.file}:{}), detail:e.detail ?? ''})), covered, planned, acs: [...] }\n    console.log(JSON.stringify(out, null, 2))\n  } else {\n    for (const e of errors) console.log(`FAIL ${e.id} ${e.ac || e.file || '-'} ${e.detail || ''}`.replace(/\\s+$/,''))\n    if (errors.length) console.log(`check-traceability: FAILED (${errors.length} errors)`)\n    else console.log(`check-traceability: ok (covered=${covered} planned=${planned})`)\n  }\n  process.exit(errors.length ? 1 : 0)\n```\nA4-06: `($r.Out | ConvertFrom-Json)` — the output must be parseable JSON only. With --json we print only JSON. ✓ But note `$r.Out` includes stderr too; we print nothing to stderr on this path. ✓\n\nA4-06 expects ok False and ids sorted unique = shard.duplicate,shard.not_owner. ✓\n\nA4-01: last non-empty line = ok line ✓.\n\nEntries order for `acs` output: index order.\n\nNow: exit code with process.exit while console.log may not flush? On Windows, process.exit can truncate stdout when piped. Safer: use `process.exitCode = n` and return, letting node flush. I'll use process.exitCode.\n\nCareful: for `--json` output through PowerShell, fine.\n\nAlso for run-tests and others, I'll prefer process.exitCode over process.exit where possible. But in run-tests after spawning we need to exit with child's code; setting process.exitCode works.\n\nHmm, but in run-tests, after the child exits, the parent's event loop may have nothing else; setting exitCode is enough.\n\nLet me write check-traceability now.\n\nOne more: `extractTitles` must handle the `platformTest` call whose first arg is a title. The regex includes platformTest. Good.\n\nAlso must the regex be exported? Yes.\n\nImplementation with matchAll.\n\nPotential regex issue: `((?:\\\\.|(?!\\4).)*)` — inside a character-level alternation, `\\4` backreference inside a negative lookahead. Works.\n\nLet me write the file.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b64c02387d24acacbd4a13028c46","_messageId":"01a1b64eaf3f7273ac32c8e7b3a093f1","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b64c02387d24acacbd4a13028c46","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b64eaf3f7273ac32c8e7b3a093f1","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b64eaf3f7273ac32c8e7b3a093f1","codebuddy.ai/messageId":"01a1b64e-af3f-7273-ac32-c8e9050d56b1","traceparent":"00-1491732b1efa8226a936b146e12cfead-98331ebc19389995-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b64b-fe4d-73dd-9fb2-26f680d0b3a8,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b64c02387d24acacbd4a13028c46"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T14:00:04.604Z"}
