ltered run writes a filtered manifest and skips the comparison', () => {\n  ...\n  assert.equal(manifest.mode, 'filtered')\n  assert.deepEqual(manifest.files, ['test/unit/a.test.mjs'])\n})\n```\n\ncheck-test-discovery selftest:\n```js\ntest('[AC-001] check-test-discovery self-test passes', () => {\n  const result = run([CHECK_DISCOVERY])\n  assert.equal(result.status, 0, result.stdout + result.stderr)\n  assert.match(result.stdout, /check-test-discovery: ok/)\n})\n```\n\nplatformTest skip record:\n```js\ntest('[AC-001] platformTest records a cliworker.skip/v1 line when the requirement is not met', () => {\n  const dir = tmp()\n  const probe = path.join(dir, 'probe.test.mjs')\n  const gateUrl = pathToFileURL(path.join(ROOT, 'test', 'helpers', 'platform-gate.mjs')).href\n  fs.writeFileSync(probe, [\n    `import { platformTest } from ${JSON.stringify(gateUrl)}`,\n    `platformTest('[probe] skip record', { ac: 'AC-001', requires: 'not-a-platform', file: import.meta.url, reason: 'probe skip' }, () => { throw new Error('must not run') })`,\n    '',\n  ].join('\\n'))\n  const result = run([probe], { env: { ...process.env, CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR: dir } })\n  assert.equal(result.status, 0, result.stdout + result.stderr)\n  const lines = fs.readFileSync(path.join(dir,'skips.jsonl'),'utf8').trim().split('\\n')\n  assert.equal(lines.length, 1)\n  const record = JSON.parse(lines[0])\n  assert.equal(record.schema, 'cliworker.skip/v1')\n  assert.equal(record.ac, 'AC-001')\n  assert.equal(record.requires, 'not-a-platform')\n  assert.equal(record.file, 'probe.test.mjs')  // relative to cwd? \n```\nCareful: recordSkip computes relativeFile using CLIWORKER_ROOT ?? cwd. The probe is in tmp dir; cwd is ROOT (spawn default cwd = process.cwd() = ROOT). So path.relative(ROOT, tmp/probe.test.mjs) = '../..'-heavy. Not 'probe.test.mjs'. I'll assert `record.file.endsWith('probe.test.mjs')` and that it contains no backslashes... Actually it'd be '../../Users/.../probe.test.mjs'. Ends with 'probe.test.mjs' ✓.\n\nAlso assert typeof record.at === 'string' && !Number.isNaN(Date.parse(record.at)), record.platform === process.platform, record.node === process.version.\n\nNote: running a file directly with `node probe.test.mjs` where the file only calls platformTest — node's test runner auto-runs. Exit status 0 for skipped test. \n\nHmm, but the probe file's test is skipped → is the process exit code 0? Yes, skips aren't failures.\n\nBut there's a catch: `platformTest` calls `recordSkip` (which needs CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR) and t.skip. ✓\n\nNow [AC-002]:\n```js\ntest('[AC-002] check-ci accepts the committed workflow and rejects every kind of drift', () => {\n  const dir = tmp()\n  fs.mkdirSync(path.join(dir, 'scripts'), { recursive: true })\n  fs.mkdirSync(path.join(dir, '.github', 'workflows'), { recursive: true })\n  const matrixSrc = path.join(ROOT, 'scripts', 'ci-matrix.json')\n  const ciSrc = path.join(ROOT, '.github', 'workflows', 'ci.yml')\n  fs.copyFileSync(matrixSrc, path.join(dir,'scripts','ci-matrix.json'))\n  fs.copyFileSync(ciSrc, path.join(dir,'.github','workflows','ci.yml'))\n  const clean = run([CHECK_CI, '--root', dir])\n  assert.equal(clean.status, 0, clean.stdout + clean.stderr)\n  ...\n```\nThen mutate:\n1. append '# x' to ci.yml → ci.stale\n2. remove one node version → matrix_invalid\n3. perf.blocking true → matrix_invalid\n4. delete windows os → matrix_invalid\n5. one character change → stale\n\nRestore ci.yml between each.\n\nImplementation: helper `writeJson(file, obj)`.\n\nCareful: after mutating ci.yml with '# x', restore from ROOT copy.\n\nAlso assert ci.missing: delete ci.yml → ci.missing. Design mentions ci.missing; nice to test.\n\n[AC-003]:\n```js\ntest('[AC-003] check-fast stops at the first failing step and propagates its exit code', () => {\n  const dir = tmp()\n  const log = path.join(dir, 'log.txt')\n  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(dir, 'check-a.mjs'), \"import fs from 'node:fs'; fs.appendFileSync(process.env.CW_LOG, 'a\\\\n')\\n\")\n  ...\n```\nCareful: env var name CW_LOG — tests set it. But writing `process.env.CW_LOG` in generated code is fine.\n\nUse JSON.stringify for paths.\n\n```js\n  const env = { ...process.env, CW_LOG: log, CLIWORKER_TEST_CHECK_DIR: dir, CLIWORKER_TEST_FINAL_SCRIPT: path.join(dir,'final.mjs') }\n  const result = run([CHECK_FAST], { env })\n  assert.equal(result.status, 7)\n  assert.match(result.stderr + result.stdout, /check-b\\.mjs/)\n  assert.equal(fs.readFileSync(log,'utf8').trim(), 'a\\nb')\n```\n\nHmm `.trim()` → 'a\\nb'. \n\nThen success case in a second dir:\n```js\n  const dir2 = tmp()\n  ... check-a (appends a), check-b (appends b, exit 0), check-c (appends c), final (appends final), check-fast.mjs decoy (appends SELF)\n  result.status === 0, log === 'a\\nb\\nc\\nfinal'\n```\nThis proves self-exclusion and ordering.\n\npwsh test via platformTest requires 'pwsh':\n```js\nplatformTest('[AC-003] the pwsh wrapper forwards the same exit code', { ac: 'AC-003', requires: 'pwsh', file: import.meta.url }, () => {\n  const dir = tmp()\n  ... failing dir ...\n  const result = spawnSync('pwsh', ['-NoProfile','-File', path.join(ROOT,'scripts','check-fast.ps1')], { encoding:'utf8', env })\n  assert.equal(result.status, 7)\n  assert.match(result.stdout + result.stderr, /check-b\\.mjs/)\n  // and .ps1 line count\n  const lines = fs.readFileSync(path.join(ROOT,'scripts','check-fast.ps1'),'utf8').split('\\n').filter(l => l.trim() && !l.trim().startsWith('#'))\n  assert.ok(lines.length <= 3)\n})\n```\n\nCareful: spawnSync('pwsh', ...) on Windows requires shell? No, spawnSync with a command name uses CreateProcess and PATH lookup; pwsh is an .exe → fine. If pwsh missing, we skip via platformTest.\n\nNow, an important consideration: inside `npm test`, CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE=1 and CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR=<dir>. The [AC-003] test dirs are separate; check-fast spawns check-a/b/c with inherited env (has CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE) but they're not run-tests, so fine. The final script is final.mjs, not run-tests. ✓\n\nBut careful in the success case: final.mjs is our own script. ✓\n\nNow [AC-002] check-ci spawn inherits env; no issue.\n\nOne risk: `run()` uses spawnSync with child output captured. For the check-test-discovery selftest which itself spawns run-tests → output captured internally. Fine.\n\nAnother risk: writing to `test/unit/runner-selftest.test.mjs` the literal `[AC-001]`, `[AC-002]`, `[AC-003]` in titles ✓.\n\nNow traceability.test.mjs.\n\n```js\nimport test from 'node:test'\nimport assert from 'node:assert/strict'\nimport fs, os, path\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process'\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'\nimport { validateIndex, mergeShards, extractTitles } from '../../scripts/check-traceability.mjs'\n\nconst HERE, ROOT, CHECK = path.join(ROOT,'scripts','check-traceability.mjs')\nconst INDEX_FILE = path.join(ROOT,'contracts','spec','ac-index.json')\nconst FIXTURES = path.join(ROOT,'test','fixtures','traceability')\n```\n\nTests:\n1. `[AC-004] the committed index is valid (74 entries, unique owners, compatible baselines)` → validateIndex(JSON.parse(...)) deepEqual [].\n2. `[AC-004] index.count catches missing, duplicated and reordered entries` — mutations.\n3. `[AC-004] index.owner` / milestone / levels / baseline / schema / baseline_incompatible.\n4. `[AC-004] shard fixtures` — spawn CLI over 6 dirs.\n5. `[AC-004] extractTitles ignores skipped titles` and finds platformTest.\n\nA4-07 requires strings: index.count, index.owner, index.milestone, index.levels, index.baseline, index.baseline_incompatible, index.schema, shard.missing_title. All present if I write the assertions with those literal ids.\n\nLet me structure with a clone helper:\n```js\nfunction cloneIndex() { return JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(INDEX_FILE,'utf8')) }\nfunction ids(errors) { return errors.map(e => e.id) }\nfunction entry(index, ac) { return index.acs.find(e => e.ac === ac) }\n```\n\nTests:\n```js\ntest('[AC-004] the committed index validates cleanly', () => {\n  assert.deepEqual(validateIndex(cloneIndex()), [])\n})\n\ntest('[AC-004] index.count rejects missing, duplicated and reordered entries', () => {\n  const missing = cloneIndex(); missing.acs.pop()\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(missing)).includes('index.count'))\n  const duplicate = cloneIndex(); duplicate.acs[73] = { ...duplicate.acs[0] }\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(duplicate)).includes('index.count'))\n  const reordered = cloneIndex(); [reordered.acs[0], reordered.acs[1]] = [reordered.acs[1], reordered.acs[0]]\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(reordered)).includes('index.count'))\n})\n```\nCareful with the duplicate mutation: replaces AC-074 with a copy of AC-001 → count mismatch and duplicates. ✓\n\n```js\ntest('[AC-004] index.owner and index.milestone reject unknown values', () => {\n  const owner = cloneIndex(); entry(owner,'AC-001').owner = 'T-1'\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(owner)).includes('index.owner'))\n  const milestone = cloneIndex(); entry(milestone,'AC-001').milestone = 'M6'\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(milestone)).includes('index.milestone'))\n})\n```\nCareful: changing AC-001's milestone to M6 also breaks graph? validateIndex doesn't do graph. But does it break count? No. ✓\n\n```js\ntest('[AC-004] index.levels and index.baseline reject empty and unknown values', () => {\n  const levels = cloneIndex(); entry(levels,'AC-001').levels = []\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(levels)).includes('index.levels'))\n  const dupLevels = cloneIndex(); entry(dupLevels,'AC-001').levels = ['unit','unit']\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(dupLevels)).includes('index.levels'))\n  const baseline = cloneIndex(); entry(baseline,'AC-001').baseline = ['beos']\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(baseline)).includes('index.baseline'))\n  const dupBaseline = ... ['linux','linux']\n})\n\ntest('[AC-004] index.baseline_incompatible rejects code levels on real-smoke and smoke on win32', () => {\n  const realSmoke = cloneIndex(); entry(realSmoke,'AC-001').baseline = ['real-smoke']\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(realSmoke)).includes('index.baseline_incompatible'))\n  const smoke = cloneIndex(); entry(smoke,'AC-038').baseline = ['win32']\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(smoke)).includes('index.baseline_incompatible'))\n})\n\ntest('[AC-004] index.schema rejects missing and extra keys', () => {\n  const missingKey = cloneIndex(); delete entry(missingKey,'AC-001').baseline\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(missingKey)).includes('index.schema'))\n  const extraKey = cloneIndex(); entry(extraKey,'AC-001').note = 'x'\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(extraKey)).includes('index.schema'))\n  const badSchema = cloneIndex(); badSchema.schema = 'nope'\n  assert.ok(ids(validateIndex(badSchema)).includes('index.schema'))\n})\n```\nCareful: deleting baseline also triggers index.baseline (undefined) — fine, we only check inclusion of index.schema.\n\nHmm: `delete entry(...).baseline` — deleting from the object inside the array works since entry returns a reference. ✓\n\nShard fixtures via CLI:\n```js\ntest('[AC-004] shard fixtures report duplicate, not-owner, missing-file, missing-title and baseline errors', () => {\n  const cases = [\n    ['ok', 0, null],\n    ['duplicate', 1, 'shard.duplicate'],\n    ['not-owner', 1, 'shard.not_owner'],\n    ['missing-file', 1, 'shard.missing_file'],\n    ['missing-title', 1, 'shard.missing_title'],\n    ['baseline-in-shard', 1, 'shard.baseline_forbidden'],\n  ]\n  for (const [name, status, id] of cases) {\n    const result = spawnSync(process.execPath, [CHECK, '--root', path.join(FIXTURES,name), '--index', path.join(FIXTURES,'ac-index.json')], { encoding: 'utf8' })\n    assert.equal(result.status, status, `${name}: ${result.stdout}${result.stderr}`)\n    if (id) assert.match(result.stdout, new RegExp(id.replace('.','\\\\.')))\n  }\n})\n```\nBetter: `--json` for precise id checks. But keep simple. Let me use --json and parse → robust.\n\n```js\n    const result = spawnSync(..., '--json')\n    const payload = JSON.parse(result.stdout)\n    assert.equal(payload.ok, status === 0)\n    if (id) assert.ok(payload.errors.some(e => e.id === id), `${name} should report ${id}: ${JSON.stringify(payload.errors)}`)\n```\nAnd for duplicate also assert exactly the two ids:\n```js\n  const dup = spawnSync(... duplicate ... --json); const p = JSON.parse(dup.stdout)\n  assert.deepEqual([...new Set(p.errors.map(e=>e.id))].sort(), ['shard.duplicate','shard.not_owner'])\n```\nThat's A4-06's requirement; good to test.\n\nextractTitles:\n```js\ntest('[AC-004] extractTitles marks skipped titles and still reads platformTest titles', () => {\n  const source = [\n    \"test('[AC-004] plain', () => {})\",\n    \"test.skip('[AC-004] skipped', () => {})\",\n    \"describe('[AC-004] group', () => {})\",\n    \"platformTest('[AC-004] gated', { requires: 'linux' }, () => {})\",\n    \"myTest('[AC-004] not a test call')\",\n  ].join('\\n')\n  const titles = extractTitles(source)\n  assert.deepEqual(titles.map(t => t.kind), ['plain','skip','plain','plain'])\n  assert.ok(titles.every(t => t.title.includes('[AC-004]')))\n})\n```\nCareful: \"myTest('...')\" — the lookbehind `(?<![\\w.])` prevents matching 'Test(' preceded by 'y'... Actually the regex would try to match at 'Test' inside 'myTest(' — the char before 'T' is 'y' (word char) → blocked. But could it match at 'myTest'? The alternation requires literal `test`/`it`/`describe`/`platformTest` case-sensitively; 'myTest' contains 'Test' (capital) — no match for lowercase 'test'. So no match. But wait 'it' — no. OK.\n\nHmm, but `assert.deepEqual(titles.map(...), [...])` — the 5th line yields nothing. Good.\n\nCareful: the file test/unit/traceability.test.mjs will contain the literal '[AC-004]' many times — that's fine, it's the file mapped for AC-004.\n\nBut caution: does traceability.test.mjs contain '[AC-001]'? If it does, then `--ac AC-001` would also select it — A1-10 only checks inclusion of runner-selftest, so fine. But A4-07 requires `--ac AC-004` → files exactly