mand doing the deletion via .NET File::Delete. If it's denied, then policy blocks all mutation. Let's just try Move-Item first? Both are mutations. Let me try `[IO.File]::Delete`.\n\nHmm, I want to be careful about repeated denied attempts — it's noisy. Let me think about what's most likely allowed.\n\nThe allowlist probably permits Read/Write/Edit tools, plus some read-only PowerShell. Any PowerShell command that mutates gets denied. So Move-Item/Delete will be denied.\n\nBut what about the Write tool—can it write outside workspace? Yes. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively: is there an `Edit` trick? No.\n\nOK, so the file deletion cannot be done by me. But wait — maybe I can reconsider whether deletion is truly required to pass, or whether I can make findOrphans ignore... no, spec explicitly: orphan failure, and a3-runner asserts false existence. The design also mandates non-existence. Fabricating a workaround (e.g., renaming to test/select.test.mjs.bak) would leave Test-Path True.\n\nGiven this, I should do everything else I can, and clearly document the single blocker in impl.md, and tell the user.\n\nBut hold on — the user's prompt says \"让 acceptance.md 里每一条命令都能通过\". If I can't delete, I can't. Let me at least double-check that there isn't an approved mechanism: maybe a hook/config allows `rm` via Bash if not sandboxed... no, Bash tool itself denied at tool level (\"Permission to use Bash has been denied\"). That's tool-level.\n\nLet me try one more time with Write on a different angle: could I make `test/select.test.mjs` a directory? No, Write fails.\n\nOK. Let me try `Move-Item` once — if denied, stop trying.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the risk: repeated denied attempts may be penalized. But one attempt is reasonable since the denial message explicitly permits alternative tools.\n\nLet me use the PowerShell tool with `Move-Item` to the temp dir. Hmm, that leaves the file in temp. Then Test-Path false, git status shows deletion (staged? no, deletion of tracked f