{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b659-7510-772d-a986-124aa4509b1f","session_id":"01a1b657-8513-7f8d-b094-21aa6d97e675","message":{"id":"01a1b6597510772da9861248e6bf91cd","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Read outside worktree denied. So sandbox = worktree only. But is .collab/blueprint symlinked inside? No.\n\nCan I read via PowerShell with Get-ChildItem? Also likely denied (path outside). The earlier PowerShell denials might be due to that too. But the Remove-Item and Move-Item were within worktree, denied → mutation denied.\n\nHmm, `Get-Content -Raw .collab/blueprint/...` denied probably because path outside sandbox.\n\nSo I cannot inspect task-graph.json. I must rely on the design's stated data (owner/milestone table) which ac-index already matches, and on check-traceability's graph parser being compatible with the blueprint. The previous dev presumably wrote checkTaskGraph based on the design's description (\"owner = 在 acceptance 里列了该 AC 的那个任务，milestone = 该任务的 milestone，levels 按集合比较\"). But the actual JSON keys could be `acceptance` per task as design says. Design §6.2 explicitly says: owner = the task that lists this AC in `acceptance`, milestone = that task's milestone. So `graph.tasks[].acceptance` is right.\n\nFor levels, design says \"levels 按集合比较\" but doesn't specify where levels come from. The implementation uses `graph.acceptance[]` array with `{id, levels}`. If the blueprint has a top-level `acceptance` array of objects with `id`/`levels`, good. The design's phrase \"levels 与任务图一致\" and \"acceptance-matrix\" suggests there is such structure. But it might instead be in `graph.tasks[].acceptance` as objects, or a separate `acceptance` map.\n\nRisk: if `graph.acceptance` doesn't exist, `levels` map is empty and `levels.has(entry.ac)` false → no levels check → still passes A4-02 (which only requires exit 0 and no mismatch). That's benign for acceptance! A4-02 only requires no graph.mismatch. If structure differs in owners, we'd get mismatch and fail.\n\nIf `graph.tasks` doesn't exist, owners empty → every AC mismatch → A4-02 fails. So the structure must have tasks with acceptance. That's the most likely blueprint shape (design refers to \"任务图\" and \"在 acceptance 里列了该 AC 的那个任务\").\n\nHmm, but maybe the task-graph uses `\"acceptance\": [\"AC-001\", ...]` per task — likely. I'll accept.\n\nNow, the main blocker is deleting test/select.test.mjs. Since I cannot, let me consider another angle: maybe the sandbox prevents shell but Write tool works. Is there a way to make test/select.test.mjs \"not exist\" for Test-Path? No.\n\nSo: I'll do a full static review, fix what I can, update impl.md honestly.\n\nBut wait — maybe I should reconsider: is `test/select.test.mjs` actually tracked? If it's an untracked leftover... doesn't matter.\n\nGiven the constraints, are there other real bugs to fix? Let me check encoding/CRLF/BOM via PowerShell read-only (bytes) — earlier ReadAllBytes-ish command worked? V-04 uses ReadAllBytes. Let me try a read-only script that lists files with CR or BOM. If PowerShell denies, I'll trust Write tool.\n\nActually, I can use the Read tool but it won't show CR. Hmm.\n\nLet me try PowerShell with a simple loop.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e2e","_messageId":"01a1b6597510772da9861248e6bf91cd","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e2e","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b6597510772da9861248e6bf91cd","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b6597510772da9861248e6bf91cd","codebuddy.ai/messageId":"01a1b659-7510-772d-a986-124aa4509b1f","traceparent":"00-c9f450d58f0fb13eec445f55f5c82b2d-7ad42e08d980766c-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id