covery with no --root → selftest → includes real repo findOrphans. During `npm test`, test/select.test.mjs absent after deletion → ok.\n\nNow check-fast step check-test-discovery runs selftest → real findOrphans ok; fixture runs. fine.\n\nNow, one important potential failure: `check-test-discovery` selftest step 1 requires enumerateTestFiles(REPO_ROOT) to have no fixtures/helpers/perf prefixes. Fine.\n\nAnother potential problem: `run-tests` in `check-test-discovery.runRunner` runs with cwd inherited (the repo root) but `--root` fixture; run-tests spawns node with cwd: root (fixture). Good.\n\nNow: A1-05 uses `$o` which was created by NewTmp. Then A1-02 reads \"$o/files-executed.json\" — wait A1-01 sets `$o = NewTmp` and runs `npm test` (default outdir!) then `run-tests --out-dir $o`. So manifest is in $o. Good. But `npm test` writes to default outdir (temp), not repo. Good.\n\nThen A1-03 reuses $o with filtered run → overwrites manifest. Good.\n\nNow A1-04: `run-tests --ac foo --out-dir $o` → exit 2, and it should not have deleted manifest? Doesn't matter. Then `--ac AC-999` → filtered; enumerates; no file contains [AC-999] → prints `FAIL run.no_match AC-999`, exit 1. Good.\n\nPotential issue: `run-tests` parseArgs for `--ac foo`: value 'foo' fails AC_PATTERN → error → usageError exit 2. Good.\n\nNow A1-12: `npm run perf` → run-tests --perf. exit 0 with 'no perf tests'. But: nested protection? `--perf` returns before that check. Good. It doesn't mkdir outDir. Good, no files-executed.json written.\n\nNow V-01: after npm test, repo must have no new files. run-tests default outDir = os.tmpdir. Good. But `npm test` might create `node_modules`? No, npm doesn't install.\n\nWait: `npm test` — npm might create no files. Fine.\n\nNow check-ci staleness: renderCi output vs ci.yml must be byte-identical. Let me manually compare renderCi against ci.yml.\n\nrenderCi lines:\n1 `# generated by ...`\nname: ci\non: [push, pull_request]\npermissions:\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  check:\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        include:\n          - name: ubuntu\n            runs_on: ubuntu-latest\n            node: 20\n...\n    runs-on: ${{ matrix.runs_on }}\n    env:\n      CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR: ${{ github.workspace }}/test-results\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: ${{ matrix.node }}\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run check\n      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n        if: always()\n        with:\n          name: test-results-${{ matrix.name }}-node${{ matrix.node }}\n          path: |\n            test-results/files-executed.json\n            test-results/skills.jsonl  ← no, skips.jsonl\n          if-no-files-found: warn\n  perf:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    continue-on-error: true\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: 22\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run perf\n\nci.yml matches exactly (as read). Note the matrix order in ci.yml ubuntu 20/22, windows 20/22, macos 20/22 = renderCi loops os then node. Yes.\n\n`  contents: read` indentation 2 spaces. Yes.\n\nNote `on: [push, pull_request]` — in YAML 1.1, `on` is boolean true, but check is byte comparison, fine.\n\nNow gen-ci writes with fs.writeFileSync(target, text) — UTF-8 no BOM, LF. ci.yml was presumably generated (or hand-written identically). It appears identical.\n\nLet me verify there's a trailing newline in ci.yml: Read showed 58 lines and file ends with run perf. renderCi returns lines.join('\\n') + '\\n'. ci.yml Read line 58 was `      - run: npm run perf` and no line 59 shown, so trailing newline likely there.\n\nNow check-ci: `ci.perf_blocking` checks `expected.includes('continue-on-error: true')` — expected always includes since renderCi hardcodes. So this check can never fail. But acceptance doesn't test ci.perf_blocking directly (A2-04 tests matrix_invalid and stale). Fine, matches design loosely.\n\nDesign §5.3 step 4: \"生成结果里 perf 作业缺少 continue-on-error 或存在 needs: perf\". renderCi always includes. OK.\n\nNow check-traceability: the `index.schema` check for extra/missing keys uses INDEX_KEYS includes — okay.\n\nPotential subtle bug: `validateIndex` pushes `index.levels` for duplicate levels: `['unit','unit']` → duplicates ['unit'] → error. Good.\n\n`index.baseline_incompatible` for AC-038 smoke + real-smoke: hasSmoke true, hasNativeOnly false (no win32/darwin) → no error. Good. But AC-038 baseline real-smoke and levels smoke → allowed.\n\nAC-001 real-smoke (unit) → hasCodeLevel && hasRealSmoke → error. Good.\n\nAC-038 win32 → hasSmoke && hasNativeOnly → error. Good.\n\nNow `index.count`: it compares exact order to AC-001..074. `index.acs` in file is ordered. Good.\n\nBut note: for `duplicated.acs[73] = {...acs[0]}` test, index.count error appears. Good.\n\nNow `mergeShards` for real repo: T-001.json five entries. All covered with files. Titles exist? \n- AC-001/002/003 → runner-selftest has plain tests with [AC-001], [AC-002], [AC-003]. Let me verify [AC-002] plain test exists: yes `test('[AC-002] check-ci accepts the committed workflow', ...)`. [AC-003]: `test('[AC-003] check-fast stops...', ...)` plain. Good.\n- AC-004 → traceability.test.mjs has `test('[AC-004] ...')`. Yes.\n- AC-074 → milestone-gate.test.mjs `test('[AC-074] ...')`. Yes.\n\n`shard.not_owner`: T-001 is owner of all five. Good.\n\nNow `check-traceability` output line for --json includes covered/planned. A4-06 uses ConvertFrom-Json and checks ok and error ids. Payload has `ok: false` (JSON boolean). `$_.ok` → False. Good.\n\nWait: JSON printed with 2-space indent. `$r.Out | ConvertFrom-Json` fine.\n\nBut note: for duplicate fixture, runJson passes `--index FIXTURE_INDEX` and `--root fixture`. check-traceability validates index (the shared valid index) → no index errors; mergeShards → duplicate+not_owner. Good. But wait — validateIndex on fixture index: it's a copy of real index with 74 entries. Must be valid. Presumably identical. Let me check its size/content quickly (spot: does it include all records?). The test `[AC-004] shard fixtures...` runs it. If fixture index differed, index errors would add ids and the assertion `deepEqual([...new Set(ids)].sort(), ['shard.duplicate','shard.not_owner'])` would fail. Let me verify fixture index equals real index.\n\nI can compare with PowerShell Compare-Object (that worked earlier). Let me try `Compare-Object (Get-Content contracts/spec/ac-index.json) (Get-Content test/fixtures/traceability/ac-index.json)` — short-ish. Earlier Compare-Object worked.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e2e","_messageId":"01a1b65a1ee178479ae4eb85457cd616","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e2e","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b65a1ee178479ae4eb85457cd616","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b65a1ee178479ae4eb85457cd616","codebuddy.ai/messageId":"01a1b65a-1ee1-7847-9ae4-eb87f634ba54","traceparent":"00-c9f450d58f0fb13eec445f55f5c82b2d-7ad42e08d980766c-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b657-8513-7f8d-b094-21aa6d97e675,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e2e"},"__timestamp":"2026-11-07T14:12:17.087Z"}
