{"type":"assistant","uuid":"01a1b65a-764a-7ad6-841b-2c8f143f883a","session_id":"01a1b657-8513-7f8d-b094-21aa6d97e675","message":{"id":"01a1b65a764a7ad6841b2c8dc0ad1c96","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All good.\n\nNow check remaining traceability fixture subject files (not-owner, missing-title read; baseline read). Not-owner subject exists? File list showed `not-owner/test/unit/subject.test.mjs`. Yes.\n\nNow the main blocker remains deletion of test/select.test.mjs.\n\nWait — maybe I *can* delete using the Write tool if I first... no.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider the sandbox: The Bash tool was denied at tool level (\"requires approval\"). PowerShell allowed for some commands. Maybe the allowlist includes specific command prefixes. `Remove-Item` not allowed. But maybe `Clear-Content`? Not delete.\n\nWhat about `git`? Forbidden by user and likely denied.\n\nHmm, what about using `node` to delete? Denied.\n\nI think deletion is impossible. But wait — the previous dev also couldn't. Yet the scheduler started a new dev run, implying perhaps permissions changed? Let me test a benign mutation to see if Write through PowerShell is allowed, e.g., `New-Item`? Not needed.\n\nActually, maybe I should try `Remove-Item` with `-Confirm:$false`? The denial is not a prompt — it's policy. Unlikely.\n\nLet me think about whether there's another tool: `EnterWorktree`? no. `NotebookEdit`? no.\n\nOK. Accept the blocker. But before finalizing, I should double-check whether there are other issues I can fix via Write/Edit, and whether the existing implementation passes acceptance otherwise.\n\nLet me re-examine a few subtle acceptance expectations that might still fail even after deletion:\n\n(1) A1-08 check-test-discovery selftest: step 2 runs full on ok fixture and compares manifest.files exactly to expected. Then it does NOT delete outDir for drift (no tmp). Fine.\n\nBut step 2 expected array: ['test/flows/c.test.mjs','test/integration/b.test.mjs','test/unit/a.test.mjs']. enumerateTestFiles sorts by code point: 'flows' vs 'integration' vs 'unit' → f < i < u. reporter sorts same. Good.\n\n(2) check-test-discovery `--root $d --manifest \"$d/manifest-*.json\"`: For manifest-ok it prints 'check-test-discovery: ok'. exit 0. Good.\n\n(3) A1-09 uses `$d = 'test/fixtures/discovery-selftest/drift'` (relative). `--manifest \"$d/manifest-ok.json\"` relative; check-test-discovery resolves. Good.\n\n(4) A3-06 checks output contains `==> check-test-discovery.mjs`. It's a step. But check-test-discovery takes time.\n\n(5) T-02: `npm test` output must have `# fail 0`. Node spec reporter prints \"# fail 0\". Good, assuming all tests pass.\n\nNow let me carefully consider the `[AC-003]` plain test that spawns check-fast with injected dir. In that test, `run([CHECK_FAST], {env})` — spawnSync inherits cwd = repo root. check-fast sets `cwd = path.dirname(dir)` = temp parent. Steps execute with cwd temp parent. The step files import 'node:fs' — fine.\n\n(6) runner-selftest `[AC-002]` uses `freshCiRoot` which copies workflow. check-ci --root dir → reads dir/scripts/ci-matrix.json and dir/.github/workflows/ci.yml. renderCi(matrix) equals copied ci.yml? The copied ci.yml must be exactly renderCi of the copied matrix. Since real repo passes check-ci, yes.\n\n(7) runner-selftest [AC-001] platformTest probe: probe imports platform-gate from repo. Node runs probe as a test file via `node probe` (not --test). At top level, `test(name, fn)` from node:test — when the file is run directly (not under --test), node:test supports running (it reports TAP to stdout). Actually with `node file.mjs` importing node:test and calling test(), Node runs tests automatically since v20? Yes: \"If node:test is used outside of the test runner, the tests are still executed and results printed in TAP format.\" Exit code 0 if passing/skipped.\n\nBut: `t.skip(why)` — t.skip exists on TestContext (node 18.17+). Good.\n\n(8) `recordSkip` writes to `$dir/skips.jsonl` — dir exists (mkdtemp). Good