enumeration of fixture root: test/unit/gate.test.mjs and test/characterization/defects/*.test.mjs. All exist. reporter writes files relative to CLIWORKER_ROOT=fixture root. Good.\n\nNow: `run-tests` spawned by gate inherits env from gate's parent. When gate is invoked from a test during `npm test`, env has CLIWORKER_RUN_ACTIVE=1 and CLIWORKER_RESULTS_DIR=<parent outDir>. Gate passes --out-dir <mkdtemp>, which differs → nested check passes. Good.\n\nBut if gate is invoked by `check-fast`? check-fast doesn't run milestone-gate. Fine.\n\nNow, another potential issue: When `npm test` runs `milestone-gate.test.mjs`, the gate fixture runs a nested full run. That nested run writes to a mkdtemp dir. Then deletes. Fine.\n\nNow let's think about `node --test` with explicit file list and the custom reporter: The `--test-reporter` option requires the reporter module path. Using file URL works in Node 20+? The docs: `--test-reporter` accepts a module specifier; for a file path, must be file URL or relative path. Design mandates pathToFileURL. Good.\n\nNode 20 supports multiple `--test-reporter` + destinations. Yes.\n\nNow, `--test-reporter-destination=stdout` fine.\n\nPotential issue: `files.mjs` reporter default export async generator that yields a string at end. Node writes yielded chunks to destination. For JSON reporter-like output, the spec reporter writes strings incrementally; our generator consumes source fully then yields once. That's allowed.\n\nPotential issue: Node's reporter contract expects yielded values to be strings. Yes.\n\nNow `mode` detection: `process.env.CLIWORKER_RUN_MODE === 'full' ? 'full' : 'filtered'`. Good.\n\nNow a subtle bug: In `run-tests`, when `verifyFullRun` fails it prints errors and sets exitCode 1 only if exitCode===0. If child failed AND verification failed, exit child's code. Design: \"子进程非 0 时用子进程的码\". Good.\n\nDesign step 4: \"完整运行：... 不通过则打印 FAIL ... 并退出 1，即使所有测试都通过。\" If child returned 0 and verification fails → 1. Good.\n\nNow: `run-tests` prints `run-tests: <mode> <n> files, manifest <path>` at the end. A1-01 checks `-match 'run-tests: full'`. Good.\n\nNow: A1-07 expects exit 1 and output matches discovery.orphan and test/loose.test.mjs. Code prints `FAIL discovery.orphan ${file}` where file is 'test/loose.test.mjs'. Good.\n\nNow: A1-09 manifest-filtered gets `discovery.mode_not_full` AND possibly `discovery.missing`? enumerated drift = [a,b]; manifest-filtered files = [a,b] mode filtered → only mode_not_full. Expected includes mode_not_full (they only check that). Good. exit 1.\n\nNow: A1-08 check-test-discovery selftest — after deletion, does anything else fail? Step 1 also checks enumerate has no excluded prefixes. Fine.\n\nHmm, one thing: `check-test-discovery` selftest step 2 compares `full.manifest.files` to expected; it also requires `full.result.status === 0`. The ok fixture has decoys under fixtures/helpers/perf which are not enumerated, so not executed. But wait: run-tests spawns `node --test <explicit files>`; Node's own test file discovery is bypassed when files given explicitly. Yes.\n\nNow: decoy in `test/fixtures/decoy.test.mjs` under ok fixture — not enumerated. Good.\n\nNow, `findOrphans(okRoot)` isn't called in runRunner... but run-tests full calls findOrphans(root): test top-level dirs: fixtures, helpers, integration, flows, unit, perf. fixtures/helpers/perf skipped; unit/integration/flows files enumerated. No orphan. Good.\n\nNow check `orphan` fixture: run-tests full → findOrphans finds test/loose.test.mjs (top-level file) → error. Good.\n\nSo after deletion, everything should pass.\n\nHold on — one more: check-fast runs `check-defects.mjs` (default). Real repo ledger absent → exit 0. But wait: `test/characterization/defects/a3-runner.test.mjs` exists in `test/characterization/defects/` dir, and `defects.json` absent. check-defects only looks for defects.json. Fine.\n\nNow, `check-test-discovery.mjs` runs inside check-fast AFTER check-ci and check-defects. It runs a nested full run on fixtures. Meanwhile the outer check-fast hasn't run npm test yet. Fine.\n\nNow let me reconsider the `reporter` and Node 22 behavior for a test file where ALL tests are skipped — not relevant.\n\nAnother risk: node:test `data.file` may be a URL object rather than string in Node 22? In Node 20+, `TestEvent.data.file` is a string path (or undefined). Some events have `file` as string. If it were a URL object, `typeof !== 'string'` → skipped → manifest missing files → A1-02 fails. I believe it's a string. In Node 20/22, tests report `file` as absolute path string. Yes.\n\nHmm, but with `--test` and explicit file args, event.data.file is absolute path. Good.\n\nNow what about the top-level `test:start` events for files that fail to load? Not relevant.\n\nOkay. Another angle: package-lock.json — `npm ci` requires lockfileVersion 3 and matching package.json. `npm ci` validates that package.json and lock agree (name, version, engines, license). The lock has name/version/license/engines. package.json has no dependencies. `npm ci` also requires `packages[\"\"]` to contain devDependencies etc. — absence is fine. It should work.\n\nWait: npm ci may complain \"Missing: ... from lock file\" if package.json has fields represented in lock. `main`, `exports`, `files`, `dsh` are not in lock — npm doesn't include those in lock's root package entry (it only includes name, version, license, dependencies, devDependencies, optionalDependencies, engines, bin, funding, etc.). Actually npm's lock root entry often includes `\"license\"`, `\"engines\"`, `\"dependencies\"`, `\"devDependencies\"`, `\"bin\"`. It does NOT include `files`/`exports`/`main`. So fine.\n\nBut if `npm ci` in CI... not part of acceptance (acceptance never runs npm ci). Only V-02 checks keys. Fine.\n\nNow, is `package-lock.json` required to have `\"lockfileVersion\": 3` and `\"requires\": true`? V-02 only checks packages keys. Good.\n\nNow let me look for any missing file from the design's list:\n- scripts/reporters/files.mjs ✓\n- All scripts ✓\n- contracts/spec/ac-index.json ✓\n- .github/workflows/ci.yml ✓\n- package-lock ✓\n- test/unit/select.test.mjs ✓\n- test/helpers/platform-gate.mjs ✓\n- test/characterization/defects/a3-runner.test.mjs ✓\n- test/traceability/T-001.json ✓\n- test/fixtures/** ✓\n- test/unit/{runner-selftest,traceability,milestone-gate} ✓\n- package.json scripts ✓\n\nNow check design §6.4 requires `test/fixtures/traceability/ac-index.json` a valid 74-entry index — yes (identical).\n\nNow, does `test/fixtures/traceability/missing-file` have a test file? No. Fine.\n\nNow `test/fixtures/traceability/not-owner` has subject.test.mjs with [AC-001]? Let me read it to be sure it doesn't accidentally include [AC-001] tagged with a skip etc. If title missing, extra missing_title error, but test only checks includes not_owner (fine). For duplicate fixture, the deepEqual of unique ids expects exactly duplicate+not_owner, so subject must have title and file exists. Duplicate fixture has subject.test.mjs (read? not read but exists). Let me read duplicate/not-owner subject quickly.","signature":""}],"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":null,"cache_read_input_tokens":null,"cache_creation":null,"server_tool_use":null,"service_tier":null}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"_requestId":"01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e2e","_messageId":"01a1b65ae1677d399065c3db706e377b","_meta":{"codebuddy.ai/conversationRequestId":"01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e2e","codebuddy.ai/modelRequestId":"01a1b65ae1677d399065c3db706e377b","codebuddy.ai/llmMessageId":"01a1b65ae1677d399065c3db706e377b","codebuddy.ai/messageId":"01a1b65a-e167-7d39-9065-c3dd81d7817a","traceparent":"00-c9f450d58f0fb13eec445f55f5c82b2d-7ad42e08d980766c-01","baggage":"codebuddy.session_id=01a1b657-8513-7f8d-b094-21aa6d97e675,codebuddy.conversation_request_id=01a1b65789647c7996b77c4308633e